// # How the APK gets built
//
// EAS reads this file as strict JSON first and falls back to JSON5, so these
// comments are part of the file rather than something that has to live
// somewhere else and go stale.
//
// It sits in `apps/mobile` and not at the repository root, which is what EAS
// documents for a monorepo — "All files related to EAS Build, such as eas.json
// and credentials.json, should be in the root of the app directory" — and
// every `eas` command is run from here.
//
// The BUILD still gets the whole workspace. EAS uploads from the git root, and
// `apps/mobile` is carried along as the project directory inside it, which is
// the only thing that could work: `@waymark/api-client`, `@waymark/domain` and
// `@waymark/i18n` are TypeScript source consumed through `workspace:*`, so an
// upload of this one directory could not install, let alone bundle. The
// install runs at the workspace root, and the package manager is chosen by
// which lockfile is found THERE — `pnpm-lock.yaml`, and nothing else, which is
// worth keeping true.
{
  "cli": {
    // A floor, not a pin. Everything used below has been in the eas.json
    // schema for a long time, and `npx eas-cli` fetches the current version.
    "version": ">= 16.0.0",

    // Android refuses to install an APK over one with a HIGHER `versionCode`,
    // and what it shows is "App not installed" with no reason given. Remote is
    // what makes that impossible to get wrong: EAS keeps the counter and
    // `autoIncrement` below moves it, so nobody has to remember on the evening
    // they are trying to ship something. It has been the recommended source
    // since EAS CLI 12.
    //
    // It does NOT touch `version` in `app.json`. That one is the version a
    // person reads in Android's app list, it is still written by hand, and the
    // first build seeds the remote counter from 1.
    "appVersionSource": "remote"
  },

  "build": {
    // One profile, named `production` because that is the one `eas build`
    // picks when nobody passes `--profile`.
    //
    // There is deliberately no `development` profile. A build pointed at a
    // machine on the LAN would be plain HTTP, which Android 9 and up refuse
    // without `usesCleartextTraffic`, and that is not configured — a profile
    // that cannot produce a working APK is worse than no profile at all.
    // Development is `pnpm --filter @waymark/mobile start` and a `.env`.
    "production": {
      // ## An APK, not an App Bundle
      //
      // EAS builds an `.aab` by default, because its default audience is the
      // Play Store. This one is not going there: it is a file attached to a
      // release that a person downloads and installs, and an App Bundle is not
      // installable — Google's servers are what turn one into APKs. So `apk`
      // is the entire reason this profile exists, and it is stated rather than
      // inferred: `distribution: "internal"` happens to imply it today, but
      // only while nothing else sets `buildType` or `gradleCommand`.
      //
      // `internal` is about who can get the file, not what shape it is. It
      // makes the build URL shareable, and it signs with this project's own
      // Android keystore — the same one every later build uses, which is what
      // lets the next APK install OVER this one rather than being refused as a
      // different app.
      "distribution": "internal",
      "android": {
        "buildType": "apk"
      },
      "autoIncrement": true,

      // ## Where the API address comes from, and why it is not written here
      //
      // `EXPO_PUBLIC_WAYMARK_API_URL` is baked into the JavaScript bundle.
      // Proven, not assumed: the exported Hermes bytecode contains the value
      // that was in scope, does not contain the variable's NAME, and does not
      // contain the `http://127.0.0.1:3000` fallback — which is dead code once
      // the variable is set. An APK built without it points at the phone's own
      // loopback and reaches nothing.
      //
      // So it MUST be set, and there is exactly one place it must not be: this
      // file. This repository is public. A hostname here is a hostname every
      // fork inherits, and the first APK somebody else built would send their
      // session token — and their inventory — to a stranger's server that
      // happens to be the one named in the file they did not know to edit.
      //
      // `environment` is the answer. It names an EAS environment rather than a
      // value, and the value lives on whichever Expo account runs the build:
      //
      //   eas env:set --name EXPO_PUBLIC_WAYMARK_API_URL \
      //     --value https://waymark.example \
      //     --environment production --visibility plaintext
      //
      // Once, per account. A fork runs the identical command with its own
      // address, edits nothing here, and has nothing to conflict on the next
      // time it pulls.
      //
      // `plaintext` and not `secret` on purpose, and it is not carelessness:
      // an address is not a credential, and secret-visibility variables are
      // deliberately unreadable outside the EAS servers — including while the
      // dynamic app config is resolved, which is exactly when `app.config.ts`
      // needs this one to derive the deep link host.
      //
      // And if it is missing, the build FAILS. `app.config.ts` refuses on an
      // EAS worker rather than handing back an APK that connects to nothing.
      "environment": "production",

      // The same pnpm that wrote `pnpm-lock.yaml` and the same one named in
      // `packageManager` at the repository root. The SDK 57 build image ships
      // a different patch of the same major, which would almost certainly be
      // fine; "almost certainly" is not worth it for a lockfile that is
      // installed with `--frozen-lockfile` and fails the build when it is not
      // understood. Move this when the root `packageManager` moves.
      "pnpm": "11.3.0"
    }
  }
}
